A goroutine is a lightweight thread managed by the Go runtime.

`go func() {`
    `fmt.Println("hello")`
`}()`

| Feature       | Goroutine         | OS Thread |
| ------------- | ----------------- | --------- |
| Managed by    | Go runtime        | OS        |
| Stack size    | ~2KB initial      | MBs       |
| Creation cost | Very cheap        | Expensive |
| Count         | Millions possible | Thousands |
Go uses **M:N scheduling**:
- Many goroutines mapped onto fewer OS threads.

**Go Scheduler**
- The **Go scheduler is part of the Go runtime**. It's a collection of code and data structures inside every Go program that decides **which goroutine runs on which OS thread**
- Scheduler = algorithms + queues + runtime functions
- It keeps track of:
	- all goroutines (G)
	- all OS threads (M)
	- all processors (P)
	- which goroutines are runnable
	- which are blocked
	- which OS threads are idle

**GMP Model**
The **GMP model** is the core of Go's scheduler. It explains **how goroutines are executed efficiently on OS threads**.

GMP MODEL

G = Goroutine
- Lightweight unit of execution
- Contains stack, program counter, etc.
- Many Gs can exist
- States: runnable, running, waiting/blocked

M = Machine
- Represents an OS thread
- Executes Go code
- Needs a P to execute a G
- Number of Ms is NOT limited by GOMAXPROCS

P = Processor
- Logical scheduler resource
- GOMAXPROCS determines number of Ps
- Has a local run queue of runnable Gs
- Required by M to execute Go code

Scheduler
- Selects runnable Gs and executes them on Ms
- Prefers P's local run queue
- Global run queue also contains runnable Gs
- Idle P can steal Gs from another P
- Can preempt long-running Gs

Global Run Queue
- Shared queue of runnable Gs
- Used when runtime cannot/shouldn't keep work
  only in local queues
- Local queues may offload work to it
- Ps can obtain work from it

Work Stealing
- If P has insufficient work
- It can steal runnable Gs from another P's local queue

Blocking Syscall

G1 → M1 → blocking syscall

M1 blocks
P1 is detached from M1
Another M can acquire P1
Other Gs continue executing

Network I/O
- Go runtime uses network poller
- G can wait for I/O without unnecessarily
  blocking an OS thread

IMPORTANT

GOMAXPROCS = maximum number of Ps that can
execute Go code simultaneously

GOMAXPROCS ≠ number of OS threads

M needs P to execute Go code

**Channels**
A channel in go is used for communication and synchronization between go routines

`ch := make(chan int)`
`go func() {`
    `ch <- 10`
`}()`
`value := <-ch`
`fmt.Println(value)`

